{
  "summary": "This computer's basic protection is switched on: Microsoft Defender is running with real-time protection and tamper protection enabled. The main concern is that it doesn't appear to be getting updates. Its virus definitions are 40 days old and the newest Windows update is 95 days old. Getting updates flowing again is the first thing to do. The remaining item is a quick check that you recognize an account.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Windows and Defender are not getting updates",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PATCH-STALE:03036687",
        "DEF-SIGNATURES-OLD:cd772dcf"
      ],
      "explanation": "Both findings point the same way: this computer hasn't received updates for a while. The newest Windows update installed is from 2026-08-20, which is 95 days old. Only one update is listed at all. Microsoft releases security fixes every month, so several months of fixes are probably missing, and the weaknesses they fix are publicly known. Separately, Defender's virus definitions are 40 days old. Defender itself is on and working, but it can only recognize threats it has definitions for, so it may miss anything newer than about a month and a half. These two problems may share a cause, so it makes sense to look at them together. The report shows the suggested fix for each one."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Confirm you recognize the account <computer>\\<account-2>",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "There is a local user account called <computer>\\<account-2> that has full control over the computer. This is often normal. Many people keep a separate account like this on purpose, and that is good practice. Just confirm it's one you or someone in your household set up. An account with this level of access that nobody recognizes would be a warning sign, but nothing here says this one is unknown. It was not flagged as newly added, since this scan wasn't compared with an earlier one. If you recognize it, there's nothing to do."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
